From 8de00df99da62b0fb5059d021757f2b40826db26
Subject: feat(bevy): glTF models are spawned and prepared node by node under a per-frame deadline (#471)
Origin: https://github.com/decentraland/dcl-one/commit/8de00df99da62b0fb5059d021757f2b40826db26
Date: 2026-11-12T19:06:33+03:00

* bevy-explorer: log each frame's time in the scene spawner and in glTF processing

Debug lines under the `spawn_timing` target, for frames that spend a millisecond or more in
bevy's `scene_spawner_system` or in `update_ready_gltfs`. Self-contained, so it can be
cherry-picked onto main to measure the baseline the same way.

* bevy-explorer: bevy's scene spawner writes a scene instance an entity at a time up to a deadline

`SceneSpawner::set_spawn_deadline` stops writing queued `Scene` instances once the instant has
passed and resumes on the next run, one instance after another in request order; each run writes
at least one entity. `None`, the default, spawns whole as before; dynamic scenes and `spawn_sync`
are unchanged (`Scene::write_to_world_with` is split into resources, allocation and per-entity
writes with the same behaviour).

A part-written instance's roots are attached to the parent from the first run, in the order a
whole spawn attaches them, and kept `Visibility::Hidden` until it is complete. Its parent
despawned, `despawn_instance` and `unregister_instance` despawn what it allocated; a scene
replaced or removed mid-spawn starts the instance over. `spawn_as_child_hidden` keeps an instance
hidden after it has spawned, until `reveal_instance_sync`.

* bevy-explorer: the scene spawner gets a 4 ms slice a frame, and glTFs show once processed

`scene_spawn_slice` sets the spawner's deadline 4 ms after it starts each frame (`SpawnScene`,
before `scene_spawner_system`), and none while the player is `OutOfWorld` or on headless, so the
arrival behind the loading backdrop and the server spawn whole. `update_gltf` spawns glTF
containers with `spawn_as_child_hidden`, and `update_ready_gltfs` reveals them once processed,
so bevy's materials and a model's collider meshes never show. `bevy-fork.md` and
`scheduling.md` describe both.

* bevy-explorer: glTF processing resumes a node at a time under the same per-frame deadline

`update_ready_gltfs` processes a spawned glTF's nodes (mesh hashing, collider shapes, scene
materials, names) until this frame's instantiation deadline, the one the scene spawner stops at,
and keeps its progress in `GltfProcessing` on the container: the node list, a cursor, the
collider counter (now keyed by owned names), the animation flag and the named nodes. Instances
already in progress go first; at least one node is processed a frame; the instance stays hidden
until its last node is done. Resetting or removing the container drops the progress.

`spawn_timing` also logs the frame's slowest glTF slice (time, nodes, source), so a single node
too large for the slice shows. `SceneSpawnerPlus::spawn_deadline` passes the deadline through.

* bevy-explorer: the benchmark autopilot can walk in while measuring

`--autopilot-walk-to <x,y>` (native): once measuring starts, the player moves in a straight line
from where the run arrived to the centre of that parcel at 6 m/s, facing it, then stands there for
the rest of the 90 s. Arriving more than the load distance away measures content that loads during
play: walking never raises the loading backdrop. The walk sets the position on the ground plane
each frame (after the movement systems, before the camera), so every run takes the same path;
the physics keeps the height. `benchmark.md` documents it, with a Genesis Plaza example
(`--position -12,2 --distance 100 --autopilot-walk-to 0,2`) and why a run must start on an empty
parcel or an SDK7 scene: the explorer never starts an SDK6 scene, so arriving inside one never
leaves the backdrop.

* docs(adr): glTF models are spawned and prepared node by node, up to a per-frame deadline

Records the decision, the rejected options and the measurements: walking into Genesis Plaza at
60 fps, the longest frame drops from 539 ms to 115 ms and the longest glTF processing in a frame
from 412 ms to 41 ms (medians of three native runs per build); standing at the arrival point shows
no difference, since that load happens behind the loading backdrop in both builds.

* fix(bevy): an instance despawned mid-spawn no longer waits for its parent

despawn_instance_sync dropped the instance's parent record but not its
scenes_with_parent entry, which set_scene_instance_parent_sync then
re-queued every frame, since the instance never completes. It is dropped
now, as unregister_instance does. The three cancel-mid-spawn tests also
check that the spawner forgets the parent.

* fix(bevy): glTF processing resumes past despawned light nodes, and a failed model spawns again

Processing despawns directional-light nodes. A model still in progress
re-ran instance_is_really_ready on the next frame, whose unwrap panicked
on the despawned node. A model in progress now skips that check (it
passed when processing started), and the check skips entities that are
gone, for its avatar callers too.

A material failure removed GltfProcessing but left GltfLoaded, so the
container waited for its despawned instance forever instead of retrying.
It is removed too, and update_gltf spawns the model again.

* fix(bevy): --autopilot-walk-to warns when it cannot take effect

Without --autopilot nothing walks, and without --csv the run ends once it
arrives; both were silent.

* docs(adr): hiding a model in progress covers rendering only

Processing adds colliders and triggers node by node, so a hidden model
already collides where its processed nodes are.

* refactor(bevy): spawning and glTF processing keep their progress on the entities, parents first

The spawner no longer lists a scene's entities from every archetype, and no
longer keeps that list, a cursor and the roots beside the instance's map.
Entities to write next carry a sparse-set marker: the top-level ones first,
and writing an entity passes it on to its children, so instances are
written parents first. Bevy resolves a child whose parent has no
visibility yet as visible, so the archetype order could show a child at the
origin between slices; a test with Bevy's visibility propagation covers it.

Hidden roots carry HiddenSceneRoot with their own visibility instead of a
map in the spawner; a root whose scene gives it none loses every visibility
component hiding added. unregister_instance uses stock's despawn queue.
Without a deadline, instances are written by stock write_to_world_with and
attached after writing. If no entity is marked while some are unwritten
(despawned by others, a scene changed in place), the instance is written
whole instead of panicking.

glTF processing drops its node list: a node is processed once it has a
ContainerEntity, and two filtered queries put models in progress first.

* docs(adr): spawning writes parents first and keeps its progress on the entities

* test(bevy): benchmark the spawner writing a large scene whole and in 4 ms slices

An ignored test, run in release: a 5,051-entity hierarchy spawned seven
times after a warm-up, whole and with a 4 ms deadline per run; it prints
the median total, the cost per entity, the runs and the longest run.

* fix(bevy): a cancelled queued or rootless instance is dropped, and a stuck one restarts in slices

despawn_instance (and unregister_instance) drop the instance from both spawn
queues: one still queued was written later without its parent. When no
entity is marked while some are unwritten, a despawned top-level entity
cancels the instance; otherwise it starts over in slices, and is written
whole only if it gets stuck again. Revealing restores a root's visibility
only while it still holds the spawner's Hidden, and leaves one whose scene
gives none Inherited rather than removing what something may now require.

* fix(bevy): removing a glTF container mid-processing despawns its model; repeated puts keep it

A container removed before its model was processed left the instance
hidden under it, with live colliders on the nodes processed so far; it is
despawned and unregistered now, which also cancels one still spawning. A
put of the value the model was loaded for no longer restarts it, so a
scene that re-puts every tick still sees large models finish. Containers
with nothing to process finish whatever the deadline. The slowest-slice
record is kept only when its debug log is on.

* fix(bevy): the benchmark walk ends exactly on its target and takes negative parcels

* docs(adr): cancelling, restarting and revealing as reviewed

* docs(adr): which commit the walk-in measured, the frame-time spread and the spawner benchmark

* fix(bevy): a spawn whose root was despawned completes, and one that fails leaves nothing

When a top-level entity is despawned by others mid-spawn, the entities
never written go and the instance completes with what is left, so its
ready event fires and holders of its id carry on, as with a whole spawn
whose root was despawned afterwards. An error while writing despawns the
part-written instance and forgets it before it is returned. The spawner's
docs say it serves one World, since it keeps a query on it.

* fix(bevy): a glTF's colliders wait for its reveal and survive the old model's despawn

Collider ids are (container, mesh name, index), so a model replacing a
processed one registers its colliders under the old model's ids while the
old one is still shown; despawning the old model then removed them. A
collider is now removed only by the entity that holds it. update_colliders
also skips the nodes of a container still processing, so a hidden model
neither blocks the player nor takes pointer events.

* perf(bevy): time the spawner and processing only when spawn_timing is logged

* docs(adr): colliders wait for the reveal; a rootless spawn completes; the rescan cost

* fix(bevy): the spawner knows exactly what is unwritten, so a stuck instance never restarts

Every allocated entity carries an unwritten marker until it is written,
and a second, ready marker once its parent is written. When none is ready
although some are unwritten, those whose parent was despawned go too and
the instance completes with what is left; those the hierarchy does not
reach are written in slices. Nothing starts over or is written whole, and
a written entity with no components is no longer taken for unwritten.

A whole spawn that fails is despawned and forgotten, like a sliced one.
The queue is passed over once per run instead of scanned per instance.
spawn_progress reports the queue and the instance being written.

* fix(bevy): processing keeps the end of the slice and resumes where it stopped

The spawner stops 1 ms before the 4 ms slice ends and glTF processing at
its end (ProcessingDeadline), so a queue of spawns no longer leaves
processing one node a frame. A model in progress resumes at the first
node not processed instead of scanning from its start. Only new colliders
on a model in progress wait for its reveal: a change to a registered
collider, or the container's own, applies at once instead of being lost.
GltfSource is recorded once loading is under way, so a value that could
not start loading is tried again when put again. The debug panel's
Instantiation section shows the slice, the spawner's queue and the glTF
models being processed.

* docs(adr): the split slice, exact unwritten tracking, diagnostics; current behaviour only

* fix(bevy): a ready entity whose parent was despawned is not written as an orphan

An entity made ready because its written parent did not list it could
lose that parent before its turn, and was then written pointing at a dead
entity. write_next now despawns it, as unstick does when the parent is
already gone.

* fix(bevy): a glTF value that cannot load is not retried on every identical put

GltfSource records the value last acted on, whether it loaded or not, and
a value that cannot load removes the previous GltfHandle, which would
otherwise spawn the old model again each frame. Only a different value
starts over.

* fix(bevy): a parent outside the scene is not taken for a despawned one

The entity mapper maps a parent the scene does not contain to a
placeholder that is never alive, and records it in the instance's map.
write_next and unstick looked such a parent up and found it gone, so they
despawned the second of two siblings extracted without their parent. Only
a parent that is a scene entity counts now.

* fix(bevy): no dead ids left in Children, and a spawner can spawn into another world

Writes skip relationship hooks, so an entity despawned before it was
written stayed listed in its written parent's Children. When a sliced
instance completes, such ids are pruned. The cached query of ready
entities is kept with its world's id and rebuilt for another world, which
panicked before.

* docs(adr): the spawner's markers by their names, and the Children pruning

* fix(bevy): a part-written instance stays in its world; empty Children go

The spawner refuses to continue an instance in a world other than the one
it started in, since its allocated entity ids mean nothing there. Pruning
dead children at completion allocates only when a dead child is listed and
removes a Children component it leaves empty, as Bevy's hooks do.

* fix(bevy): despawns and the spawner system check the part-written instance's world

Despawning a part-written instance (directly, from the despawn queue, or
because its parent looks gone) in another world would despawn unrelated
entities there and leak the instance's own. Those paths now panic before
anything changes, as does `spawn_queued_scenes`, whose check moves ahead
of the dynamic scenes it writes. Tests catch each panic and check that
nothing changed in either world and that the instance still completes or
despawns in its own.

* test(bevy): the cross-world tests pin the spawner's bookkeeping too

The instance under test is now a hidden child, and after each refused
call the tests check that the spawner still records its parent, its
hidden flag, the part-written instance and any queued despawn. Forgetting
the instance before the world check now fails the despawn test.

* refactor(bevy): the instantiation slice is a share of the target frame, owned by one plugin

- The slice is a quarter of the target frame (`SceneUpdates::frame_target`,
  60 fps when uncapped), as the raycast budget takes a tenth; processing
  keeps a quarter of the slice. 60 fps keeps 4.2 ms.
- `SceneSpawnSlicePlugin` owns the slice, `ProcessingDeadline`, the debug
  section and `spawn_timing`, which moves beside it as a plugin.
- `spawn_timing` measures the spawner and glTF processing into the Bevy
  diagnostics `instantiation/scene_spawner` and
  `instantiation/gltf_processing`; the debug section shows both. Processing
  records each model's slice, and spawn_timing logs the slowest.
- `GltfProcessing` comes with `GltfLoaded` and is updated in place; one
  query takes models already started first. The per-node work moves into
  `NodeProcessing::process`. `update_ready_gltfs` takes
  `Res<ProcessingDeadline>`, and checks nodes with `.contains`.
- The walk-in uses `scene_runner::parcel_to_vec3`.

* docs(adr): the walk-in re-run at the head, and the web build measured

The native walk-in re-run at the merged head measured no instantiation in
either build (Genesis Plaza's scene did not start within the run), so the
table stays the earlier measurement, labelled with its commits. The web
build's walk-in is recorded: slowest frames, what the spawner and
processing take, and that models, avatars and colliders behave. Drops the
provenance comparisons the public-prose rule excludes.

* docs(bevy): the guidelines take the lessons of this PR's review

- code-style: work that spans frames keeps one component, updated in
  place; a feature is one plugin; `Res<T>` for resources the app always
  adds; membership checks with `.contains`; per-item work in a function or
  a `SystemParam` method; `bevy::platform::time::Instant`; a refusal checks
  before it changes anything; measurements are Bevy diagnostics.
- scheduling: a per-frame budget is a share of the target frame.
- contributing: reuse helpers such as `parcel_to_vec3`; measurements
  describe the merged code; a change to per-frame work is tried in the
  web build by hand, with the worst frames reported.
- running: how to try a pull request's web build by hand.

* test(bevy): the processing tests pin the started-first order and real progress

`GltfProcessing` now comes with every loaded model, so the order test
passed with a one-pass loop and the removal test's assertion held before
any node was processed. The order test starts the later-spawned model
first (the earlier one's scene has no context yet) and checks it finishes
before the earlier one starts; it fails if the loop takes one pass. The
removal test checks that a node was processed.

* docs(bevy): narrow three of the review's guideline rules

- code-style: drop the rule on work that spans frames; it is this PR's
  fix, too specific for a general rule.
- code-style: per-item functions only for a system whose loop has grown
  too long; a short loop stays as it is.
- contributing: the manual web run is for changes that limit per-frame
  work (a budget, slicing), where it is part of the case for the change.

